# /usr/share/w/defaults/session-terminal.list — programs `w-session` may reopen
# inside a terminal window.
#
# W's own defaults. Do NOT edit: every update overwrites this file wholesale.
# Personal entries belong in ~/.config/w/session-terminal.list, which the update
# never touches; both files are read and their entries are simply added together.
#
# WHY AN ALLOWLIST
#
# `w-session` can see what is running in a terminal window — a terminal is a
# process with a child on a pty, so the program in the foreground is reachable
# through /proc. Reopening it, though, means RE-EXECUTING it at login, and what
# people run in a terminal is arbitrary: a build, a database migration, a deploy
# script, anything behind `sudo`. Coming back tomorrow to find those re-run
# because they happened to be on screen when the session ended is not session
# restore, it is a surprise with consequences.
#
# So the question this file answers is not "what did the user run" — that is
# already known — but "what is safe to run AGAIN, unattended, with no one
# looking". Editors, pagers, viewers and monitors qualify: re-running them shows
# you what you were reading and touches nothing. Everything else still gets its
# terminal back, in the same directory, which is the whole of what was lost and
# is inert until you type something.
#
# Turning the gate off entirely is `w-session terminal all` — a per-machine
# decision for someone who knows what they run.
#
# Format: one program name per line, matched (anchored) against the program's
# own name — argv[0]'s basename, not the kernel's truncated comm. Regexes are
# allowed. '#' and blank lines are ignored.

# Editors. They reopen the file named on the command line; unsaved buffers and
# cursor position are the editor's own business and do not come back.
helix
hx
micro
nvim
vim
vi
nano
kak
emacs

# Pagers and viewers — reading, never writing.
less
more
most
man
bat
glow

# System monitors. Nothing they do outlives being closed.
btop
htop
top
atop
bottom
btm
nvtop
iotop
s-tui

# File managers and browsers over the filesystem. They open on a directory, and
# `w-session` restores the working directory alongside.
yazi
ranger
nnn
lf
mc
broot

# Read-mostly TUIs over a repository or a container host. They are the reason
# the terminal was open and they act only when told to.
lazygit
gitui
tig
lazydocker
k9s

# Music and media, where "still playing what I was playing" is the point.
ncmpcpp
cmus

# Deliberately NOT here, and each for the same reason — re-running it acts on
# the machine rather than showing you something:
#   sudo, su, doas       privilege, and a password prompt at login
#   make, cargo, npm     a build that starts itself is a build you did not ask for
#   ssh, mosh            a connection re-established without you is a surprise
#   tmux, screen         they already restore their own sessions, better than we
#                        could, and reattaching from here would fight them
#   watch, entr          they re-run something else, so the same argument twice
